    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.org/sig/ont/fma/fma67498</IRI>
        <Literal>Old definition: Anatomical structure which has as its direct parts portions of two or more types of tissue or two or more types of cardinal organ part which constitute a maximally connected anatomical structure demarcated predominantly by a bona fide anatomical surface. Examples: femur, biceps brachii, liver, heart, skin, tracheobronchial tree, ovary.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.org/sig/ont/fma/fma67498</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Organ</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/sig/ont/fma/FMAID"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.org/sig/ont/fma/fma67504</IRI>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">67504</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/sig/ont/fma/definition"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.org/sig/ont/fma/fma67504</IRI>
        <Literal>anatomical structure, each instance of which is a regional part of some cardinal body part; it has as its constitutional part some subdivision of the integument, such that for each instance of cardinal body part the meronymic sum of its cardinal subdivisions constitutes the instance of cardinal body part. Examples: thorax, perineum, back of neck, forearm, hand, phalanx, nose, auricle of ear, scrotum.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
